  "_doc": "N-111: the secp256k1 EC builtins' OPERAND GATES as the compiled script enforces them (packages/runar-compiler/src/passes/ec-codegen.ts). ecAdd and ecNegate: each Point must be 64 bytes (CL-BUG-095) and each coordinate a canonical field element, x < p and y < p (R-117); off-curve operands and the all-zero identity O are accepted (R-053: O is the identity, P+O = P, P+(-P) = O), except that ecAdd ABORTS when it doubles a point with y = 0 other than O (N-110). ecMul: the point must be ON the curve or be O (R-157), which also covers width and canonicity; ecMulGen has no point operand. ecPointX / ecPointY / ecEncodeCompressed: width only, no canonicity or curve check. ecOnCurve (N-184): width + canonicity + curve-eq, all ANDed into a FLAG -- a wrong-width blob is `false`, never an abort, because the predicate must stay total for `if (ecOnCurve(p)) {...} else {...}`. Every row is the COMPILED SCRIPT's verdict on @bsv/sdk Spend.validate, asserted by packages/runar-testing/src/__tests__/n111-ec-operand-gates-onchain.test.ts (accept at `expect`, reject at a different witness; an `abort` row rejects both the eq and the ne probe; ecOnCurve's boolean `expect` runs through the same eq/ne scheme with a boolean witness). Every off-chain implementation that models these builtins (the runar-testing interpreter behind TestContract and each tier's native runtime) must replay this file and agree: the value for an accepted row, an error -- never a value -- for an `abort` row (ecOnCurve: `false`, never an error, for a wrong-width row). Fields: `fn`; `p`, `q` = Point operands as hex (64 bytes unless the row tests width); `k` = scalar as a decimal string; `expect` = hex bytes for a Point / ByteString result, a decimal string for the bigint result of ecPointX / ecPointY, \"true\"/\"false\" for the boolean result of ecOnCurve, or \"abort\". `why` is a human note.",
